  "faChokingTitle": "Choking",
  "faChokingSigns": "Pawing at the mouth, gagging, coughing, blue gums or struggling to breathe.",
  "faChokingSteps": "Stay calm and keep your pet still. If they can still cough, let them try to clear it.\nOpen the mouth gently and look inside with a torch.\nIf you can see the object and reach it easily, hook it out with your fingers. Don't push it deeper.\nSmall dog or cat: hold them upside down by the hips and give 5 sharp taps between the shoulder blades.\nLarge dog: stand behind, put your fist just below the ribs and give 5 quick pushes inwards and upwards.\nCheck the mouth again. Go to a vet straight away, even if the object came out.",
  "faChokingAvoid": "Don't reach blindly down the throat.\nDon't give food or water.\nDon't pull on string or thread you see; it may be caught on something inside.",
  "faChokingVet": "Always see a vet after choking, even if your pet seems fine. Use SOS if they collapse or can't breathe.",
  "faPoisoningTitle": "Poisoning",
  "faPoisoningSigns": "Vomiting, drooling, shaking, wobbly walking, fits, sudden tiredness, or a chewed packet nearby.",
  "faPoisoningSteps": "Move your pet away from the poison and remove any left in the mouth.\nFind out what was eaten, how much and when. Keep the packet or take a photo of the plant.\nCall your vet now, or use SOS to reach the nearest clinic.\nIf the poison is on the fur or skin, rinse it off with plenty of lukewarm water.\nKeep your pet warm and quiet on the way to the clinic.",
  "faPoisoningAvoid": "Don't make your pet vomit unless a vet tells you to.\nDon't give milk, salt, oil or home remedies.\nDon't wait for signs; some poisons act hours later.",
  "faPoisoningVet": "Go now. With poisons, early treatment saves lives.",
  "faHeatstrokeTitle": "Heatstroke",
  "faHeatstrokeSigns": "Heavy panting, thick drool, bright red or pale gums, weakness, vomiting or collapse on a hot day.",
  "faHeatstrokeSteps": "Move your pet into shade or a cool room right away.\nPour cool (not ice-cold) water over the body, especially the belly, neck and paws.\nUse a fan or wet towels, and change the towels often so they don't trap heat.\nOffer small sips of cool water; don't force it.\nGo to a vet even if your pet seems better.",
  "faHeatstrokeAvoid": "Don't use ice or ice-cold water.\nDon't cover your pet with a wet towel and leave it there.\nDon't walk in the afternoon heat, and never leave a pet in a parked car.",
  "faHeatstrokeVet": "Heatstroke can harm organs hours later. See a vet today. Flat-faced breeds are at higher risk.",
  "faBleedingTitle": "Bleeding",
  "faBleedingSigns": "Blood from a wound, paw, ear or nose.",
  "faBleedingSteps": "Press a clean cloth or gauze firmly on the wound.\nKeep pressing for at least 5 minutes without lifting to check.\nIf blood soaks through, add more cloth on top and keep pressing.\nWrap a bandage firmly, but not too tight, to hold it in place.\nKeep your pet calm and go to a vet.",
  "faBleedingAvoid": "Don't tie a tight band (tourniquet) unless a vet tells you to.\nDon't pull out anything stuck in the wound.\nDon't put turmeric, powders or creams on a deep wound.",
  "faBleedingVet": "Use SOS for heavy bleeding, spurting blood or bleeding that doesn't slow after 10 minutes of pressure.",
  "faFractureTitle": "Broken bones",
  "faFractureSigns": "Not putting weight on a leg, swelling, a strange angle, crying when touched.",
  "faFractureSteps": "Keep your pet as still as possible. A frightened pet may bite, so be gentle.\nCarry small pets in a box or basket lined with a towel.\nFor a large dog, use a board or a blanket held like a stretcher.\nCover any open wound with a clean cloth.\nGo to a vet straight away.",
  "faFractureAvoid": "Don't try to straighten the bone.\nDon't give human painkillers; many are poisonous to pets.\nDon't let your pet walk on the leg.",
  "faFractureVet": "Every suspected broken bone needs a vet, and usually an X-ray.",
  "faSeizureTitle": "Fits (seizures)",
  "faSeizureSigns": "Falling over, stiff or jerking legs, paddling, drooling, not responding.",
  "faSeizureSteps": "Stay calm and note the time the fit started.\nMove furniture and sharp things away. Dim the lights and keep it quiet.\nKeep your hands away from the mouth.\nAfter the fit, keep your pet warm and let them rest; they may be confused for a while.\nCall your vet and tell them how long it lasted.",
  "faSeizureAvoid": "Don't hold your pet down.\nDon't put anything in the mouth; pets don't swallow their tongue.\nDon't give food or water until they are fully awake.",
  "faSeizureVet": "Use SOS if a fit lasts more than 5 minutes or fits come one after another. A first fit always needs a vet visit.",
  "faBurnTitle": "Burns",
  "faBurnSigns": "Red or raw skin, blisters, singed fur, or contact with hot liquid, fire or chemicals.",
  "faBurnSteps": "Cool the burn under cool running water for 10 minutes.\nFor a chemical burn, rinse with plenty of water for 15 minutes; wear gloves.\nCover loosely with a clean, damp, lint-free cloth.\nKeep your pet warm by wrapping the rest of the body, and go to a vet.",
  "faBurnAvoid": "Don't use ice, butter, oil, toothpaste or turmeric.\nDon't pop blisters.\nDon't let your pet lick the burn.",
  "faBurnVet": "Burns can be deeper than they look. See a vet the same day.",
  "faBiteTitle": "Snake or insect bite",
  "faBiteSigns": "Sudden swelling, pain, puncture marks, bleeding, weakness, drooling or trouble breathing.",
  "faBiteSteps": "Keep your pet calm and as still as possible; carry them if you can.\nKeep the bitten part level with or below the heart.\nTake off the collar if the neck or face is swelling.\nFor a bee sting, scrape the sting out with a card.\nGo to the nearest clinic straight away. Use SOS to find one that's open.\nIf it's safe, note the snake's colour and size, or take a photo from a distance.",
  "faBiteAvoid": "Don't cut the wound or try to suck out venom.\nDon't tie a tight band above the bite.\nDon't try to catch the snake.",
  "faBiteVet": "Every snake bite is an emergency. Go even if your pet seems fine.",
  "faTickTitle": "Removing a tick",
  "faTickSigns": "A small grey-brown bump stuck to the skin, often around the ears, neck, armpits or between the toes.",
  "faTickSteps": "Wear gloves if you can. Part the fur so you can see the tick clearly.\nUse fine tweezers or a tick hook to grip the tick close to the skin.\nPull straight out, slowly and steadily, without twisting or squeezing.\nClean the spot with antiseptic and wash your hands.\nDrop the tick in alcohol, or seal it in tape before throwing it away.\nWatch for fever, tiredness or pale gums over the next 3 weeks.",
  "faTickAvoid": "Don't burn the tick or cover it in oil, kerosene or petrol.\nDon't squeeze the tick's body.\nDon't crush ticks with bare fingers.",
  "faTickVet": "See a vet if your pet gets a fever, is tired or off food, or has many ticks. Ask about tick prevention.",
