  "toxChocolateName": "Chocolate",
  "toxChocolateWhy": "Has theobromine and caffeine, which cause vomiting, a racing heart and fits. Dark chocolate and cocoa powder are the most dangerous.",
  "toxGrapesName": "Grapes and raisins",
  "toxGrapesWhy": "Even a few grapes or raisins (kishmish) can cause sudden kidney failure in dogs and cats. Birds and rabbits: only a tiny piece as a rare treat.",
  "toxOnionGarlicName": "Onion and garlic",
  "toxOnionGarlicWhy": "Raw, cooked or powdered, and in curries and gravies too, they damage red blood cells and cause weakness days later.",
  "toxXylitolName": "Xylitol (sugar-free sweetener)",
  "toxXylitolWhy": "Found in sugar-free gum, mints, some peanut butters and medicines. In dogs it causes a sudden drop in blood sugar and liver damage. Keep it away from all pets.",
  "toxAlcoholName": "Alcohol",
  "toxAlcoholWhy": "Even small amounts cause vomiting, wobbliness, breathing problems and coma. Raw bread dough with yeast also makes alcohol in the stomach.",
  "toxCaffeineName": "Tea, coffee and caffeine",
  "toxCaffeineWhy": "Tea, coffee, energy drinks and caffeine tablets cause restlessness, a racing heart and fits.",
  "toxAvocadoName": "Avocado",
  "toxAvocadoWhy": "Very dangerous for birds and rabbits: it can damage the heart. For dogs and cats the stone can block the gut, and the skin and leaves upset the stomach.",
  "toxCookedBonesName": "Cooked bones",
  "toxCookedBonesWhy": "Cooked chicken or mutton bones splinter and can choke your pet or tear the gut. Never give cooked bones.",
  "toxMilkName": "Milk",
  "toxMilkWhy": "Most adult cats and dogs can't digest milk well and get loose motions. Don't give milk to birds or rabbits.",
  "toxSaltySnacksName": "Salty snacks (namkeen, chips)",
  "toxSaltySnacksWhy": "Namkeen, chips and papad cause thirst and vomiting; large amounts can cause salt poisoning. Birds are especially sensitive.",
  "toxSpicyFoodName": "Spicy food",
  "toxSpicyFoodWhy": "Chilli and masala upset the stomach and cause vomiting and loose motions. Curries usually contain onion and garlic too.",
  "toxFattyFoodName": "Fried and fatty food",
  "toxFattyFoodWhy": "Fried snacks, ghee, fat trimmings and rich leftovers can cause pancreatitis, a painful swelling of the pancreas.",
  "toxSweetsName": "Sweets (mithai)",
  "toxSweetsWhy": "Sugary sweets cause weight gain and tummy upsets, and many contain ghee, chocolate, raisins or sugar-free sweeteners.",
  "toxFruitSeedsName": "Fruit stones and seeds",
  "toxFruitSeedsWhy": "Mango stones, apple seeds and the stones of plums, peaches and cherries can choke or block the gut, and some contain cyanide.",
  "toxTomatoName": "Tomato",
  "toxTomatoWhy": "A little ripe tomato is usually fine. Green tomatoes, leaves and stems are harmful.",
  "toxPeanutsName": "Peanuts",
  "toxPeanutsWhy": "Only plain, unsalted peanuts, a few at a time. Salted or masala peanuts are not safe, and peanut butter must not contain xylitol.",
  "toxMangoName": "Mango",
  "toxMangoWhy": "Ripe mango flesh is fine as a small treat. Remove the skin and the stone. Too much causes loose motions.",
  "toxAppleName": "Apple",
  "toxAppleWhy": "Apple slices without the seeds and core are a healthy, crunchy treat.",
  "toxBananaName": "Banana",
  "toxBananaWhy": "A small piece is a fine treat. It is high in sugar, so keep it small.",
  "toxWatermelonName": "Watermelon",
  "toxWatermelonWhy": "Seedless pieces without the rind make a cooling summer treat.",
  "toxCarrotName": "Carrot",
  "toxCarrotWhy": "Raw or cooked carrot is a healthy treat. For rabbits, keep carrot as a small treat, because it is sugary.",
  "toxRiceName": "Plain cooked rice",
  "toxRiceWhy": "Plain rice with no salt, ghee, onion or masala is gentle on the stomach in small amounts. Rabbits need hay, not rice.",
  "toxEggName": "Cooked egg",
  "toxEggWhy": "A fully cooked egg is a good protein treat. Avoid raw egg. Rabbits eat only plants.",
  "toxLilyName": "Lilies",
  "toxLilyWhy": "Every part of a lily, even the pollen or the vase water, can cause kidney failure in cats. Keep lilies out of homes with cats.",
  "toxSagoPalmName": "Sago palm (cycas)",
  "toxSagoPalmWhy": "Every part is poisonous, the seeds most of all. It causes vomiting and liver failure.",
  "toxOleanderName": "Oleander (kaner)",
  "toxOleanderWhy": "Oleander affects the heart. A few leaves or flowers can be fatal.",
  "toxCastorName": "Castor plant (arandi)",
  "toxCastorWhy": "Castor seeds contain ricin. Chewing even one or two seeds can be fatal.",
  "toxDaturaName": "Datura (dhatura)",
  "toxDaturaWhy": "The seeds and flowers cause a racing heart, wide pupils, confusion and fits.",
  "toxLantanaName": "Lantana",
  "toxLantanaWhy": "The berries and leaves of this common hedge plant can damage the liver.",
  "toxAloeVeraName": "Aloe vera",
  "toxAloeVeraWhy": "The clear gel is usually fine, but the bitter yellow sap under the skin causes vomiting and loose motions.",
  "toxMoneyPlantName": "Money plant",
  "toxMoneyPlantWhy": "Chewing the leaves causes mouth pain, drooling and vomiting.",
  "toxSnakePlantName": "Snake plant",
  "toxSnakePlantWhy": "Chewing it can cause drooling, vomiting and loose motions.",
  "toxMarigoldName": "Marigold (genda)",
  "toxMarigoldWhy": "Chewing marigolds can cause mild mouth irritation and an upset stomach.",
  "toxTulsiName": "Tulsi (holy basil)",
  "toxTulsiWhy": "Tulsi is not known to be poisonous to pets. Large amounts may still upset the stomach.",
  "toxArecaPalmName": "Areca palm",
  "toxArecaPalmWhy": "A pet-friendly houseplant.",
  "toxSpiderPlantName": "Spider plant",
  "toxSpiderPlantWhy": "Not poisonous, though cats love to chew it and may vomit a little.",
  "toxRatPoisonName": "Rat poison",
  "toxRatPoisonWhy": "Causes internal bleeding or fits, sometimes days later. Take the packet to the vet.",
  "toxHumanMedicinesName": "Human medicines",
  "toxHumanMedicinesWhy": "Paracetamol and painkillers like ibuprofen are poisonous to pets. One paracetamol tablet can kill a cat. Give only medicines your vet prescribes.",
  "toxMosquitoRepellentName": "Mosquito coils and vaporisers",
  "toxMosquitoRepellentWhy": "The fumes can harm birds' lungs. Use them away from pets and keep the room aired.",
  "toxMothballsName": "Naphthalene balls",
  "toxMothballsWhy": "Poisonous if chewed, and the fumes harm birds. Keep them in closed cupboards.",
  "toxNonStickFumesName": "Non-stick pan fumes",
  "toxNonStickFumesWhy": "An overheated non-stick pan gives off fumes that can kill a bird within minutes. Keep birds out of the kitchen.",
  "toxPhenylName": "Phenyl and floor cleaners",
  "toxPhenylWhy": "Phenyl and many floor cleaners contain phenols, which are especially poisonous to cats. Let floors dry before pets walk on them.",
